topics=[]
def topic(id,title,group,summary,why,detail,evidence,trap,example,terms,related,source,slides,poster,steps=None):
 topics.append(dict(id=id,title=title,group=group,summary=summary,why=why,detail=detail,evidence=evidence,misconception=trap,example=example,terms=terms,related=related,source=source,slides=slides,poster=poster,steps=steps or [],support='Faithful paraphrase; plain-language explanations are educational interpretations.'))
topic('rationale','Why chromosome 9?','1 · Foundations','This study describes chromosome 9 annotation patterns across public acute leukemia sequencing runs.','The chromosome contains leukemia-associated loci, but a gene label alone does not establish a pathogenic alteration.','The introduction connects chromosome 9 to leukemia research through PAX5 and neighbouring loci. The study asks how recovered gene assignments and coding summaries compare across heterogeneous public runs. It describes annotations rather than establishing a clinical test or a causal disease mechanism. Literature findings reported in the manuscript provide context, not validation of these particular records.','The manuscript discusses PAX5-related literature and reports PAX5-associated records in both AML and ALL summaries.','A known leukemia-associated gene makes every record assigned to it pathogenic. Gene relevance and the effect of a particular allele are different questions.','PAX5 in both groups means both recovered annotation sets contain PAX5 assignments. It does not establish that the same allele occurs in both.',['chromosome9','pax5','annotation','allele'],['design','annotation','conclusions'],'Introduction; Background and Literature Review; §3.2.2',[2,5],'Introduction and Objective; Results and Discussion')
topic('design','What kind of study is this?','1 · Foundations','A secondary descriptive analysis of six sequencing runs, not a review article or a recruited patient cohort.','The analytical unit determines which conclusions are defensible. Six runs are not six independent patients.','The authors retrieved public DNA sequencing inputs and compared annotation profiles. The sequencing run was the analytical unit. The study did not estimate patient mutation prevalence, clinical performance or between-group statistical significance. The manuscript contains a literature review section, but that section does not make the whole study a systematic or narrative review.','Six runs represented four BioSample identifiers. The three ALL runs shared one REH source.','Three ALL runs and three AML runs mean two independent clinical cohorts of three patients each.','Treating different platforms measuring REH as independent patients would exaggerate biological replication.',['run','biosample','descriptive','replicate'],['provenance','limitations','calling'],'§3.1.1; §3.1.4; §3.2.1',[3],'Materials and Methods')
topic('provenance','Six runs, four biological sources','1 · Foundations','The ALL group repeats one REH BioSample. AML inputs differ in biological material and assay purpose.','A profile can differ because of the source or assay, even when the disease label is unchanged.','ALL-1, ALL-2 and ALL-3 share SAMN13831871 and use PromethION, PacBio Sequel II and Illumina HiSeq X. AML-1 is ML-2 from a control/decitabine study. AML-2 is an HL-60 apoptotic DNA-fragment library after 24 hours at room temperature. AML-3 is bone-marrow DNA studied by sparse sequencing for copy-number quantification. These inputs are not biologically or technically equivalent.','Table 1 identifies the run accessions and four BioSamples. The three AML BioSamples are SAMN02709918, SAMN34434777 and SAMN17383328.','The label AML makes all three AML assays interchangeable.','The HL-60 fragment library captures apoptosis-related DNA fragments rather than the same measurement design as dense genomic small-variant profiling.',['run','biosample','reh','library','coverage'],['design','processing','limitations'],'§3.1.1; Table 1; §3.2.1',[3],'Materials and Methods', ['Identify the biological material.','Identify the library purpose and sequencing platform.','Check whether runs share a BioSample.','Interpret differences within that context.'])
topic('processing','From public reads to an alignment','2 · Methods','Quality assessment, trimming, assembly checks and chromosome-focused alignment precede annotation.','Downstream annotation depends on which sequences survive processing and how well they represent the reference.','The reported workflow uses FastQC in Galaxy, Trimmomatic, SPAdes and QUAST. The manuscript states Q30 filtering, a 50 bp minimum read length and retained contigs of at least 500 bp. Reads and assembly-derived sequences were imported into Geneious and aligned to chromosome 9 at medium sensitivity. Platform-specific command settings and complete intermediate files are not supplied, so the paper is not a fully executable pipeline specification.','Reference: GRCh38.p14 chromosome 9, NC_000009.12.','Naming several tools proves every platform received an optimal or identical valid workflow.','The presence of long-read and short-read platforms is a reason to ask how processing was adapted to each input.',['read','q30','contig','reference'],['alignment','calling','provenance'],'§3.1.2',[4],'Materials and Methods',['Retrieve sequences and inspect quality.','Trim adapters and low-quality sequences.','Assemble and assess assembly statistics.','Align reads and assembly-derived sequences to chromosome 9.','Transfer reference annotations and examine coverage.'])
topic('alignment','Identity is not coverage','2 · Methods','ALL-1 had 91.4% pairwise identity but only 0.8% chromosome 9 reference coverage.','Good agreement in a small observed region does not tell us what lies in the unobserved remainder.','Pairwise identity concerns sequence agreement within an alignment. Reference coverage concerns how much of the chromosome reference is represented. ALL-1 had a reported maximum coverage of 3. Sparse representation restricts claims about absence of annotations elsewhere. The manuscript also reports 328,321 reads entering the analysis, with 326 assigned to the assembled component and 327,995 unassembled. Do not interpret reference feature counts as detected mutations.','ALL-1: identity 91.4%; reference coverage 0.8%; maximum coverage 3.','High pairwise identity means the whole chromosome has been adequately sampled.','Think of checking a small paragraph against a book: a good match in that paragraph is not evidence that every page was checked. This is an analogy, not a study measurement.',['identity','coverage','reference','callable'],['overlap','limitations','processing'],'§3.2.1; Discussion',[4],'Materials and Methods', ['Ask how well aligned sequences agree.','Separately ask how much reference was observed.','Restrict absence claims to adequately observed regions.'])
topic('calling','Calling thresholds versus group tests','2 · Methods','Geneious filter settings are not tests of AML–ALL differences.','A small calling P-value cannot establish disease-group enrichment.','The manuscript reports an approximate minimum variant frequency of 0.6, a maximum variant P-value below 0.001 and a strand-bias P-value threshold of 10⁻⁶. Strand bias above 65% was flagged. These settings were used for detection and filtering. Their precise software implementation must be checked in the analysis records before reproducing the workflow. All between-group comparisons were descriptive.','No between-group significance test, confidence interval or clinical performance estimate is reported.','P < 0.001 in the calling method means AML has significantly more mutations than ALL.','A setting governing whether an individual candidate is retained answers a different question from a test comparing two groups.',['variantfrequency','strandbias','pvalue','descriptive'],['design','annotation','counts'],'§3.1.3; §3.1.4',[4,5],'Materials and Methods')
topic('annotation','Annotation records versus validated variants','2 · Methods','An annotation record is an annotated entry used in the summaries, not automatically an independent somatic mutation.','Counting records, assigning genes and proving pathogenicity are different analytical tasks.','Reference annotations assign context such as genes and coding sequences to aligned differences. Multiple records can relate to the same locus or allele. Gene assignment does not establish a fusion, functional disruption or clinical classification. The reference layer contained 2,487 gene features, 5,514 coding-sequence features, 5,505 messenger RNA features, 1,636 noncoding RNA features and 6,270 regulatory features. Those are reference features, not counts of altered genes or expressed transcripts.','PAX5 entries were present in both groups; NUP214 in ALL and NOTCH1 in AML summaries. The study does not establish specific pathogenic lesions or fusions.','A reference gene feature count equals the number of variant-bearing genes.','A file label pointing to NUP214 is not proof of a NUP214 fusion.',['annotation','somatic','pax5','transcript','allele'],['coding','counts','limitations'],'§3.1.3; §3.2.1–§3.2.2; Discussion',[5],'Results and Discussion')
topic('counts','What do the annotation counts show?','3 · Results','AML-associated counts had a higher median and wider range, with overlap between groups.','Describe the observed pattern without converting it into a prevalence estimate or significance claim.','The ALL run-level range was 4–4,943 and its median 92. The AML range was 25–8,117 and its median 1,822. Each group has three sequencing runs, but the ALL runs share one source. These are annotation-record counts. With heterogeneous assays and sparse coverage, a larger record count cannot be read directly as greater biological disease burden.','ALL median 92, AML median 1,822; ranges overlap. Figure 1 displays the distributions.','The bigger AML median proves a significant difference or greater clinical mutation burden.','A defensible oral sentence is: “AML-associated record counts were higher and more dispersed in this collection, with overlapping ranges.”',['median','annotation','descriptive','replicate'],['provenance','calling','limitations'],'§3.2.2; Figure 1',[5],'Results and Discussion')
topic('coding','Protein effects and amino-acid summaries','3 · Results','Substitutions predominated in both groups. Truncation labels were more prominent in ALL.','Predicted consequence labels suggest follow-up questions but do not prove a protein-level mechanism.','Proline-to-leucine led the ALL-associated top-ten amino-acid-change summary, while glycine-to-arginine led AML. Proline-to-leucine, glutamate-to-lysine, alanine-to-valine and alanine-to-threonine appeared in both top-ten lists. These categories are pooled across coding sequences and do not identify a specific protein domain or recurrent allele. Sequence-change classes are a separate annotation layer and should not be conflated with protein-effect categories.','Figures 3 and 4 show protein-effect and amino-acid categories. Exact numerical chart data are not supplied in a machine-readable table.','A frequent pooled amino-acid change identifies a common functional driver in one gene.','Proline-to-leucine can occur in unrelated proteins. A shared category is not necessarily a shared genomic alteration.',['substitution','truncation','frameshift','transcript'],['positions','annotation','conclusions'],'§3.2.3–§3.2.4; Figures 3–4',[6],'Results and Discussion')
topic('positions','Why pooled positions are not hotspots','3 · Results','Codon and coding-position labels are pooled across coding sequences, not chromosome coordinates.','The same number can refer to positions in many different proteins or transcripts.','The leading codon-number labels were 151 in ALL and 539 in AML. The leading coding-sequence-position labels were 1074 and 1327, respectively. To establish recurrence, one would need a normalized alteration in a specified gene and transcript across independent samples. Table 2 lists selected position categories in ascending numerical order rather than frequency order.','ALL: leading codon 151 and coding-position 1074. AML: 539 and 1327.','The most prominent pooled codon label is a chromosome 9 mutation hotspot.','Codon 151 in one gene and codon 151 in another are different molecular positions.',['codon','transcript','hotspot','allele'],['coding','annotation','limitations'],'§3.2.4; Table 2; Discussion',[6],'Not individually displayed')
topic('overlap','Shared and group-restricted sets','3 · Results','Few recovered labels or annotation records were shared between the two pooled groups.','A recovered-set difference describes these data; it does not establish biological exclusivity.','Gene-label overlap comprised 196 AML-only, 15 shared and 41 ALL-only labels. Variant annotation-record overlap comprised 742 AML-only, 4 shared and 551 ALL-only entries. These are separate set comparisons with different units. Sparse coverage, different assay purposes and annotation handling may contribute to low overlap. Do not derive a proportion without specifying which set and denominator it refers to.','Figure 5: gene labels 196 / 15 / 41; annotation records 742 / 4 / 551 (AML-only / shared / ALL-only).','AML-only entries are validated leukemia-specific biomarkers or proven absent from ALL.','“Group-restricted in the recovered profiles” is more accurate than “unique to all AML genomes.”',['set','coverage','callable','annotation'],['alignment','clustering','limitations'],'§3.2.5; Figure 5; Discussion',[7],'Results and Discussion')
topic('clustering','Why clustering does not separate the groups','3 · Results','AML and ALL labels intermix. ALL-3 and AML-2 form the closest pair.','A dendrogram summarizes similarity in a chosen feature representation, not diagnostic accuracy.','ALL-1, ALL-2 and AML-3 joined the ALL-3/AML-2 pair successively, while AML-1 joined last. Correlation distance and average linkage were used. Branch height represents clustering distance, not statistical support. Mixed labels do not imply that AML and ALL are genomically indistinguishable. Biological source, assay design, coverage and feature processing all limit interpretation.','Figures 6 and 7 both pair ALL-3 with AML-2.','The closest pair has the same biology, or the dendrogram proves that leukemia types are indistinguishable.','One ALL run can resemble an AML run in the recovered annotation matrix without erasing biological distinctions between leukemia types.',['dendrogram','correlation','linkage','heatmap'],['heatmap','overlap','limitations'],'§3.1.4; §3.2.6; Figures 6–7',[8],'Results and Discussion')
topic('heatmap','Reading the clustered heat map','3 · Results','Rows are six runs, columns are genes, and values show the plotted annotation representation.','Colour is meaningful only with its scale and preprocessing, not as a direct clinical measure.','The manuscript reports row-wise unit-variance scaling, missing-value imputation, correlation distance and average linkage. The figure caption describes a displayed 0–1 colour scale. Those statements do not fully document how standardized values became the plotted range. The exact transformation and imputation details need the analysis code or original matrix. Do not infer raw counts, expression or disease severity from colour alone.','Figure 7 intersperses AML and ALL labels and pairs ALL-3 with AML-2.','A green cell means increased gene expression, or a displayed 0–1 value is necessarily a raw mutation frequency.','A colour scale is a visual encoding of processed matrix values. Ask what operation produced those values before assigning a biological interpretation.',['heatmap','scaling','imputation','correlation'],['clustering','annotation','limitations'],'§3.1.4; §3.2.6; Figure 7 caption',[8],'Clustering summarized; heat map not displayed')
topic('limitations','What the evidence cannot establish','4 · Appraisal and Defence','The study supports descriptive annotation comparisons, with substantial design and coverage limitations.','Good discussion depends as much on the boundary of a claim as on the observed numbers.','The collection is small, its three ALL runs share a source, and the AML assays differ. ALL-1 reference representation is sparse. Records may not be independent alleles. There is no matched normal comparison, functional validation or clinical outcome analysis. These issues restrict leukemia-specific, mechanistic and biomarker conclusions. Source accession transparency and a defined reference are strengths, but do not remove those limits.','The discussion explicitly calls for independent samples, suitable platform-specific processing, justified callable regions and allele-level normalization.','Listing limitations means the descriptive findings have no value. Their value is bounded, not erased.','A useful contribution is a traceable chromosome-focused description that motivates more controlled comparisons.',['replicate','somatic','callable','biomarker'],['design','alignment','conclusions'],'Discussion; Conclusion',[9],'Conclusion; Materials and Methods')
topic('conclusions','A defensible oral synthesis','4 · Appraisal and Defence','Different recovered profiles, limited overlap and mixed clustering support further controlled study.','A concise synthesis should connect the observations to their limits and a realistic next step.','Explain the purpose, identify the six-run/four-BioSample design, describe the count and overlap patterns, then state why the findings remain descriptive. Do not describe a validated leukemia-specific signature, a causal mechanism or a clinical biomarker. Recommend independent biological sources, adequate callable coverage and normalized gene/transcript-level alleles before functional or clinical validation.','The conclusion reports variable chromosome 9 profiles, predominant substitutions, limited set overlap, mixed run-level clustering and PAX5 records in both groups.','The title must be defended as a discovery of disease-specific pathogenic variants.','“We describe heterogeneous chromosome 9 annotation profiles in public leukemia-associated runs. Provenance and coverage limit disease-level interpretation.”',['biomarker','annotation','callable'],['limitations','overlap','counts'],'Discussion; Conclusion',[9],'Conclusion')
terms=[]
def term(id,name,full,plain,why,mechanism,remember,source,related,topic,origin=None):
 terms.append(dict(id=id,term=name,full=full,plain=plain,why=why,mechanism=mechanism,remember=remember,source=source,related=related,topic=topic,origin=origin or 'The manuscript does not provide a historical naming explanation.',support='Educational definition in the manuscript context, not a verbatim source quotation.'))
term('chromosome9','Chromosome 9','','The chromosome reference selected for this comparison.','It anchors every gene assignment to the same chromosome context.','Sequences are aligned to GRCh38.p14 chromosome 9, accession NC_000009.12.','Same reference, different recovered profiles.','§3.1.2',['reference','pax5'],'rationale')
term('pax5','PAX5','','A gene label discussed in the leukemia literature and found in both study groups.','Gene relevance does not identify which alteration is present.','Annotation associates a record with a gene; allele-level analysis is needed to characterize the alteration.','Gene name is context, not a verdict.','Introduction; §3.2.2',['annotation','allele'],'rationale')
term('annotation','Annotation record','','An annotated entry included in the descriptive summaries.','The unit counted may differ from a distinct, validated mutation.','The workflow attaches gene and coding context to aligned differences.','Record count is not automatically mutation count.','§3.1.3',['somatic','allele'],'annotation')
term('allele','Allele','','A particular version of sequence at a specified locus.','Two records with the same gene label need not describe the same allele.','Reference and alternate sequences must be related to a defined genomic and transcript context.','Which change, at which site?','§3.1.3; Discussion',['transcript','annotation'],'annotation')
term('run','Sequencing run','','One sequencing data acquisition identified by a run accession.','This study uses runs as analytical units.','Several runs may measure the same biological material using different platforms.','A run is a measurement, not necessarily a new sample.','§3.1.1; Table 1',['biosample','replicate'],'design')
term('biosample','BioSample','','An identifier describing the biological source of archived sequence data.','Repeated runs can share the same source.','The three ALL runs all refer to SAMN13831871.','Count sources separately from runs.','Table 1',['run','reh'],'provenance')
term('reh','REH','','The leukemia cell-line source represented by all three ALL runs.','The ALL group does not contain three independent biological sources.','One BioSample was measured with PromethION, PacBio Sequel II and Illumina HiSeq X.','Three platforms, one REH source.','§3.1.1',['biosample','replicate'],'provenance')
term('replicate','Biological replication','','Independent biological sources, as distinct from repeated measurement of one source.','Independent replication is needed for disease-population generalization.','Repeated platform measurements of REH do not create new independent patients.','More runs need not mean more biology.','§3.1.1; Discussion',['run','biosample'],'design')
term('library','Sequencing library','','The prepared sequence material measured by a sequencing experiment.','Different preparation goals can recover different genomic information.','An apoptotic DNA-fragment library and sparse copy-number sequencing answer different measurement questions.','Preparation shapes what you can observe.','§3.1.1',['coverage','run'],'provenance')
term('read','Read','','A sequence observation obtained by sequencing.','Read quality and alignment influence which reference regions are represented.','The study processes reads and assembly-derived sequences before annotation.','Observed sequence first, interpretation later.','§3.1.2',['contig','coverage'],'processing')
term('q30','Q30','Phred quality score of 30','The quality-filtering threshold described in the methods.','It is a processing setting, not a guarantee of chromosome completeness.','The reported trimming uses Q30 and a minimum read length of 50 base pairs.','Quality does not replace coverage.','§3.1.2',['read','coverage'],'processing')
term('contig','Contig','','An assembled contiguous sequence used in the reported processing workflow.','Assembly-derived sequences help form the alignment input.','SPAdes assembly retained contigs of at least 500 base pairs.','Assembled stretch of sequence.','§3.1.2',['read','reference'],'processing')
term('reference','Reference sequence','','The sequence used as the alignment and annotation coordinate context.','Comparisons require a defined reference.','The study uses GRCh38.p14 chromosome 9, NC_000009.12.','A coordinate framework, not a patient measurement.','§3.1.2',['annotation','coverage'],'alignment')
term('identity','Pairwise identity','','Agreement between the sequences being aligned.','It describes agreement in observed alignments, not the whole reference.','ALL-1 reports 91.4% identity alongside only 0.8% reference coverage.','Match quality and observed extent are different.','§3.2.1',['coverage','reference'],'alignment')
term('coverage','Reference coverage','','How much of the chromosome reference is represented by the aligned sequences.','Poor representation weakens claims about biological absence elsewhere.','ALL-1 covers 0.8% of chromosome 9; maximum coverage is separately reported as 3.','How much did we actually observe?','§3.2.1',['identity','callable'],'alignment')
term('callable','Callable region','','A region sufficiently observed for a justified variant comparison.','Absence is interpretable only where the assay could reasonably detect the alteration.','Future comparisons should use justified callable regions rather than assuming uniform chromosome representation.','No observation is not proof of no variant.','Discussion',['coverage','allele'],'limitations')
term('variantfrequency','Variant-frequency setting','','A calling setting used to retain candidate differences in Geneious.','It governs calling, not between-group statistical significance.','The approximate minimum setting reported is 0.6. Exact implementation requires the original workflow.','Caller rule, not cohort result.','§3.1.3',['pvalue','strandbias'],'calling')
term('strandbias','Strand bias','','Uneven representation of a candidate difference across sequence strands, considered during filtering.','It is a quality concern rather than evidence of an AML–ALL effect.','The methods give a P-value threshold of 10⁻⁶ and flag bias above 65%.','Check evidence symmetry before interpretation.','§3.1.3',['variantfrequency','pvalue'],'calling')
term('pvalue','Calling P-value','','A statistical quantity used within the variant-calling/filtering procedure.','It must not be reused as a group-comparison P-value.','The method reports a maximum variant P-value below 0.001, while group comparisons remain descriptive.','Ask which question the P-value belongs to.','§3.1.3–§3.1.4',['descriptive','strandbias'],'calling')
term('descriptive','Descriptive analysis','','A summary of observed patterns without the reported between-group significance testing or clinical-performance estimation.','It sets the language appropriate for the conclusions.','Counts, categories, overlaps and similarity patterns are summarized for this collection.','Describe the collection before generalizing.','§3.1.4',['median','replicate'],'design')
term('somatic','Somatic variant','','A sequence alteration arising in somatic cells, distinct from inherited variation.','These annotation records have not all been established as clinically classified somatic variants.','The study lacks a matched normal comparison and discusses tumor-only interpretation limits.','Annotation alone does not prove somatic origin.','§3.1.3; Discussion',['allele','biomarker'],'annotation')
term('transcript','Transcript context','','The RNA/coding-sequence reference context used to describe a predicted effect.','The same genomic change can need different interpretation depending on the transcript used.','The manuscript calls for gene- and transcript-level allele normalization before interpreting recurrence.','Name the transcript before naming the consequence.','Background; Discussion',['codon','allele'],'positions')
term('median','Median','','The middle value after ordering the observations.','It summarizes these three-run groups without proving a population difference.','Reported medians are 92 in ALL and 1,822 in AML.','Middle, not average.','§3.2.2',['descriptive','annotation'],'counts')
term('substitution','Predicted substitution','','A predicted protein-effect category predominant in both groups.','A category label does not establish functional validation.','The manuscript distinguishes predicted protein effects from experimentally established effects.','Predicted label first, validation later.','§3.2.3',['truncation','frameshift'],'coding')
term('truncation','Predicted truncation','','An annotation predicting a shortened protein product.','Truncation labels were more prominent in ALL, but remain computational predictions.','The meaning depends on the affected allele and transcript.','A predicted shorter product, not a proven phenotype.','§3.2.3',['transcript','substitution'],'coding')
term('frameshift','Frameshift','','A coding consequence that changes how downstream codons are read.','It is one of the predicted protein-effect categories in the study.','Its biological impact requires the specific allele and coding context.','Shift the reading frame, then inspect the context.','§3.1.3; §3.2.3',['codon','truncation'],'coding')
term('codon','Codon label','','A position label within a coding sequence, pooled here across coding sequences.','A pooled label is not a unique chromosome coordinate.','Codon 151 can occur in more than one gene.','A number needs a gene and transcript.','§3.2.4; Table 2',['hotspot','transcript'],'positions')
term('hotspot','Mutational hotspot','','A recurrent alteration region requiring defined molecular context and suitable independent evidence.','Pooled coding-position labels cannot establish one.','The discussion asks for normalized changes in specified genes/transcripts across independent samples.','Recurrence needs a precise address.','Discussion',['codon','allele'],'positions')
term('set','Annotation set','','A collection of recovered labels or records used for overlap comparison.','Gene-label sets and record sets count different things.','Figure 5 compares AML-only, shared and ALL-only membership at both levels.','Specify what a set contains.','§3.2.5; Figure 5',['annotation','callable'],'overlap')
term('dendrogram','Dendrogram','','A tree showing the order and distances of clustering merges.','Branch height is not a significance level or a clinical score.','ALL-3 and AML-2 merge first in this analysis.','A similarity tree, not a diagnosis.','§3.2.6; Figure 6',['linkage','correlation'],'clustering')
term('correlation','Correlation distance','','The similarity-related distance used for the reported clustering.','The chosen representation and distance affect what “close” means.','The methods report correlation distance, not a clinical similarity metric.','Close in this matrix, under this rule.','§3.1.4',['linkage','scaling'],'clustering')
term('linkage','Average linkage','','The merge rule used to build the reported hierarchical clustering.','It helps define the dendrogram topology.','The study combines correlation distance with average linkage.','Distance plus merge rule makes a tree.','§3.1.4',['dendrogram','correlation'],'clustering')
term('heatmap','Heat map','','A coloured display of the processed run-by-gene annotation matrix.','Colour does not directly mean expression or clinical severity.','Rows are runs and columns are genes. The displayed 0–1 scale needs its preprocessing context.','Read the legend and transformation first.','§3.2.6; Figure 7',['scaling','imputation'],'heatmap')
term('scaling','Row-wise scaling','','The reported transformation of run-level profiles before heat-map analysis.','It changes the meaning of plotted values relative to raw counts.','The manuscript specifies unit-variance scaling but does not fully explain the displayed 0–1 mapping.','Transformed values are not automatically raw frequencies.','§3.1.4; Figure 7',['heatmap','imputation'],'heatmap')
term('imputation','Missing-value imputation','','Filling missing entries as part of matrix processing.','The chosen procedure can affect similarities and needs documentation.','Imputation is reported, but the complete implementation is not given.','A filled value needs a stated rule.','§3.1.4',['heatmap','scaling'],'heatmap')
term('biomarker','Clinical biomarker','','A measurable feature with evidence supporting a defined clinical use.','This study does not establish that its group-restricted annotations meet that standard.','Clinical usefulness would require suitable independent, functional and clinical validation.','A candidate pattern is a starting point.','Discussion; Conclusion',['somatic','callable'],'conclusions')
term('aml','AML','Acute myeloid leukemia','The disease-associated label used for three included sequencing runs.','The label does not make ML-2, HL-60 fragments and bone-marrow assays interchangeable.','Interpret the label alongside biological material, platform and assay purpose.','Disease label plus provenance.','§3.1.1; Table 1',['biosample','library'],'provenance')
term('all','ALL','Acute lymphoblastic leukemia','The disease-associated label used for the three REH runs.','All three runs measure one shared BioSample.','Different platforms observe the same REH biological source.','Three ALL runs, one source.','§3.1.1; Table 1',['reh','biosample'],'provenance')
term('sra','SRA','Sequence Read Archive','A public archive used to obtain the sequencing inputs.','Run accessions make the source data identifiable.','The authors retrieved sequence data using the SRA Toolkit or ENA.','Archive accessions support traceability.','§3.1.1–§3.1.2',['ena','run'],'processing')
term('ena','ENA','European Nucleotide Archive','Another archive used for the public sequence data.','It documents sequence-source provenance.','ENA is used alongside the SRA Toolkit in the retrieval workflow.','Locate the archived source before interpreting it.','§3.1.1–§3.1.2',['sra','run'],'processing')
term('geneious','Geneious','','The software used for chromosome 9 alignment, calling and annotation in the reported workflow.','Software settings affect which annotations are recovered.','The study reports medium-sensitivity mapping and specified calling/filter settings.','Tool name is not a full reproducible protocol.','§3.1.2–§3.1.3',['reference','variantfrequency'],'processing')

# One core flashcard per topic and one per glossary item.
cards=[]
for t in topics:cards.append(dict(id='fc-'+t['id'],topic=t['id'],front=t['title'],back=t['summary']+' '+t['why'],source=t['source']))
for t in terms:cards.append(dict(id='fc-term-'+t['id'],topic=t['topic'],front='Explain '+t['term']+' in this study.',back=t['plain']+' '+t['remember'],source=t['source']))
questions=[]
def mc(id,topic,prompt,options,correct,explanation,source,difficulty='Conceptual'):
 questions.append(dict(id=id,topic=topic,type='mcq',difficulty=difficulty,objective=next(t['title'] for t in topics if t['id']==topic),prompt=prompt,options=options,correct=correct,answer=options[correct],explanation=explanation,source=source,support='Source-supported question. Wrong options are deliberate distractors.'))
mc('q01','design','What is the analytical unit?',['Patient','Sequencing run','Independent hospital cohort','Clinical outcome'],1,'The methods explicitly use the sequencing run. The other options imply patient recruitment or clinical analysis that did not occur.','§3.1.1','Recall')
mc('q02','provenance','How many BioSamples do the six runs represent?',['Six','Three','Four','Two'],2,'The three ALL runs share one source; the AML runs have three other BioSamples. Six counts runs, not biological sources.','Table 1; §3.2.1','Recall')
mc('q03','alignment','What does 91.4% identity establish?',['The whole chromosome is adequately covered','Agreement within aligned sequences','91.4% of genes are mutated','Clinical validation'],1,'Identity describes alignment agreement. It does not measure chromosome extent, gene mutation prevalence or clinical validity. ALL-1 coverage is 0.8%.','§3.2.1')
mc('q04','calling','What does the reported P < 0.001 concern?',['AML–ALL enrichment','Patient survival','Variant detection/filtering','Clinical diagnostic accuracy'],2,'It is a caller threshold. No between-group significance test or clinical analysis is reported.','§3.1.3–§3.1.4')
mc('q05','counts','Which is the defensible count summary?',['AML has significantly more disease-causing mutations','AML-associated counts have a higher median, with overlapping ranges','ALL has no relevant variants','There are six independent patients'],1,'The descriptive record-count summary is supported. Significance, pathogenicity, absence and patient independence are not established.','§3.2.2')
mc('q06','overlap','Which are the shared gene-label and annotation-record counts?',['15 and 4','196 and 742','41 and 551','4 and 15'],0,'The shared intersection contains 15 gene labels and 4 records. Other values are group-restricted counts or reverse the units.','§3.2.5; Figure 5','Recall')
